- Input field types inside `package.manifest.inputs`: string, text, number, boolean, select, multiselect, list, json, natural_language, file.

## qcg harness vocabulary (use it to deduce the whole package)

Step types for `package.manifest.flow` (each item: id, type, params; optional needs, when, on_deps "none_failed", output, artifact):

- `write`: output_file + content (content may reference the proposed input fields).
- `render`: template + output_file (template ships via sources).
- `llm.fill`: prompt + schema (schema ships via sources); output captured via top-level `output`.
- `ask_user`: HITL question. `content`, optional `options` (single choice) or `fields_from` pointing at an array of input fields (dynamic form).
- `copy`: source + target (package-relative source).
- `transform`: json_pretty | json_compact | toml_to_json | json_to_toml | json_merge (with) | inject_secrets (secrets) | zip.
- `command`: `command` (array of strings, required — the full command including the binary; templated) — run external tools.
- `check.command`: run a host command and assert expectations. Params: `command` (array of strings, required — the full command including the binary), `expect` ({exit_code, stdout_contains, ...}) — USE THIS for validation gates like `nginx -t` after emitting a config.
- `check.container`: run a command inside a container. Params: `command` (array of strings, required — the full command including the binary), optional `image`, `mounts` ([{from, to, mode}]), `expect` ({exit_code, stdout_contains, ...}). Example: command = ["nginx", "-t"], image = "nginx:stable".
- `check.schema` / `check.format` / `check.contract`: structural validators.
- `foreach`: items (dotted path to an array or object) + subflow naming a `blocks.<name>` group; block params see the current array entry as `item`. Object entries use the generic `{key, value}` shape, exposed as `item.key` and `item.value`.

Control fields: `when` (boolean expr over inputs/steps), `on_deps` ("all_succeeded" default, "any_succeeded", "none_failed"), `needs`, `output`, `artifact` ({label, required}).

Adaptive HITL design rules:
- If an operator decision can change the workflow, declare that decision as an `ask_user` node in the DAG, give it explicit `needs`, and gate each resulting branch with `when` over `steps.<question>.output`.
- If the LLM produces a variable question array, expose it with `ask_user`'s `fields_from` pointing at that declared array so the form is generated at run time; do not hard-code one question node per possible question.
- Do not add fixed `ask_user` questions merely to simulate interaction. Ask only for information that is missing or whose answer materially changes the generated workflow or its permissions.

If the requested description mentions validating, testing, linting, compiling, or checking the output ANYWHERE, you MUST propose the `package` tier whose `manifest.flow` contains BOTH the emission step AND the matching `check.command` / `check.schema` / `check.container` gate. Never omit verification the user asked for.

Required outputs are declared ONLY as per-node `artifact = {label, required}` on flow items, or under `outputs.extras` as [{glob, label, required}]; never as a top-level `artifact` key. Permissions are never part of the proposal — the operator selects them explicitly.

